    "posts/six-months-of-gardening/index.html": {
      "text": [
        "I moved this site from a blog to a garden six months ago. Here is what actually changed.",
        "I write less and revise more",
        "Post count went down by roughly two thirds. Edit count went up by a lot more than that. The pile is smaller and better, which is the trade I wanted, but it does feel unproductive in a way a publishing schedule never did.",
        "The links do the organising",
        "I have not made a single tag decision in six months. Notes find each other because I link them while writing, and the backlink lists surface structure I did not plan — see [[Backlinks change what you write]].",
        "The surprise",
        "The most-linked note is the most boring one: [[Notes should be atomic]]. It is four paragraphs of mechanics with no insight in it at all, and nearly everything else leans on it. Apparently load-bearing ideas are rarely the interesting ones."
      ],
      "text_sha256": "6b739c3bd3097c49208e3c08337afcaae50696b960f13f7f2f4f40fb54c99887",
      "title": "Six months of gardening in public | Marginalia",
      "type": "text/html"
    },
    "posts/welcome/index.html": {
      "text": [
        "A blog is a stack of finished things in date order. A garden is a set of unfinished things that point at each other, and the pointing is the useful part.",
        "Notes here get revised in place rather than superseded by a newer post. If a note changes your mind, it will also have changed itself since you last read it.",
        "Every note shows what links to it. That list is usually more interesting than the note, because it tells you which other ideas needed this one."
      ],
      "text_sha256": "d58beffd78b2ae3da8d9e3e88b07b6334acd45eed11c91ae016696ca026dca90",
      "title": "This is a garden, not a blog | Marginalia",
      "type": "text/html"
    },
